course: dictionary
lesson: 950
title: Bloodthirsty: reading the clues
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you meet a hard word, read the words around it. These are its context, and they often give clues. «A hundred bloodthirsty badgers, armed with rifles, are going to attack Toad Hall this very night.» Armed with rifles and attacking a house are clues that these badgers want to hurt people, so bloodthirsty must mean eager to kill or hurt.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «A hundred eager-to-hurt badgers, armed with rifles, are going to attack Toad Hall» still makes sense, so your meaning fits. If the sentence stopped making sense, you would know to try again.
explain: Bloodthirsty is also used for stories and films. A bloodthirsty story has a lot of killing and violence in it. In The Wind in the Willows, the weasels are feared as bloodthirsty, so the word helps you picture danger and fear.
explain: The word is made from blood and thirsty. Here thirsty means eager for something, not wanting a drink. Knowing the parts of a word can give you a clue, but always check the dictionary when you are unsure.
fact: Bloodthirsty means eager to kill or hurt people, or enjoying violence.
fact: A bloodthirsty story or film has a lot of killing and violence in it.
fact: Bloodthirsty is an adjective, so it describes a noun such as a pirate, a tyrant, a monster or a story.
fact: Bloodthirstier is the comparative form, meaning more bloodthirsty.
fact: Bloodthirstiest is the superlative form, meaning the most bloodthirsty.
exercise: «A hundred bloodthirsty badgers, armed with rifles, are going to attack Toad Hall this very night.» What does bloodthirsty mean here? | *Eager to kill or hurt people | Covered in red paint | Loudly singing | Very tired after a long journey
exercise: The pirates in the story were bloodthirsty. What does this suggest about the pirates? | Good at maths | Kind to animals | Afraid of the sea | *Eager to hurt people
exercise: Which description fits a bloodthirsty story? | A story with no words at all | *A story with lots of killing and violence | A story about a quiet library | A story about a happy picnic
test: Which word is the comparative form, meaning more bloodthirsty? | Bloodthirstiness | Bloodthirsty | Bloodthirstily | *Bloodthirstier
test: Which word could replace bloodthirsty in «The monsters in the old tale were bloodthirsty» without changing the meaning? | Hungry for lunch | *Eager to hurt people | Shiny | Sleepy
exam: In «A hundred bloodthirsty badgers, armed with rifles, are going to attack Toad Hall this very night», what are the badgers eager to do? | *Hurt people and fight violently | Sell their rifles at a market | Sing a lullaby to Toad | Hide quietly in the paddock
